Hi Chaoyi,

You are right, and it is wider than this patch. Nothing consumes npu-supply.
There is no regulator call anywhere in drivers/accel/rocket, and grepping the
whole tree for npu-supply and sram-supply finds no reader at all, so the
RK3588 boards that set them are in the same position. The property is required
by rockchip,rk3588-rknn-core.yaml, so a dts cannot simply drop it.

Your suspicion about them being disabled is what the always-on line in this
patch is for. Without it the regulator core turns an unclaimed rail off when
it disables unused regulators, so on this board that line is the only thing
holding vdd_npu_s0 up.

The mechanism that does exist is domain-supply on the power domain node, which
rockchip_pd_power gets with devm_of_regulator_get and enables before the domain
goes on and disables after it goes off. rk3588-rock-5b-5bp-5t.dtsi already
wires the same vdd_npu that way. So the rail should follow the domain rather
than be pinned on, and v8 moves it there.

I am testing that on the board before I send it, because it changes when the
rail drops rather than only who asks for it.

One more thing to withdraw while I am here. This commit message says that
dropping the rail under the domains takes an async SError on the next power-on.
I went looking for the run that showed it and cannot find one. Every SError I
have recorded on this hardware is the power domain itself, which is what
patches 6 and 7 address, and I think I attributed it to the rail from memory.
That sentence is out in v8 whatever the domain-supply test says.

Thanks for looking at it.

Jiaxing
